on4:5 (carryover1).\n\nPosition5:2.\n\nPosition6:8.\n\nSo the result would be 8 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 7 (hundreds),6 (tens), 3 (ones). Wait but let's see:.\n\nWait the positions:.\n\nPosition6 (leftmost): 6 +2 =8.\n\nPosition5 (next):1 +0 +1 (carryover from thousands) → 1+0 is 1, plus carryover 1 → 2.\n\nPosition4 (thousands):6+9=15, so 5 and carryover1.\n\nPosition3 (hundreds):2+4=6 + no carryover (since the previous step after thousands had a carryover, but hundreds was separate. Wait, in the hundreds place, after thousands, the carryover comes from the thousands addition.\n\nWait, let me step through again carefully:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Ones place: 7 +6 =13 → 3, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens place:6 +9 =15 +1 (carryover) =16 → 6, carryover1.\n\n3. Hundreds place:2 +4 =6 +1 (carryover) →7, no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands place:6 (from first number) +9 (from second) =15 →5, carryover1.\n\n5. Ten thousands place:1 (from first) +0 (second) =1 + carryover1 →2, no carryover.\n\n6. Hundred thousands place:6 +2 =8.\n\nSo putting all digits together from right to left:</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]  5%|4         | 23/500 [09:49<3:18:09, 24.93s/it]
