at carried-over 1, the total will indeed have seven digits. So the millions place is just the carried-over 1. .\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the millions place: 1, then 7 in the hundred-thousands, 8 in the ten-thousands, 7 in the thousands, 7 in the hundreds, 7 in the tens, and 5 in the ones. Wait, hold on, did I mix up any of the digits?.\n\nLet me check again step by step. Maybe I made a mistake in the places. Let me write it out vertically:.\n\n      921126.\n    + 866649.\n    -----------.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 6 + 9 = 15 → write 5, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nTens place: 2 + 4 = 6, plus 1 is 7 → correct.\n\nHundreds: 1 + 6 = 7 → correct.\n\nThousands: 1 (from 921,126) and 6 (from 866,649). Wait, let me confirm the digits again. .\n\nWait the first number is 921,126. Breaking down each digit:.\n\n- 9 (hundred thousands).\n- 2 (ten thousands).\n- 1 (thousands).\n- 1 (hundreds).\n- 2 (tens).\n- 6 (ones).\n\nThe second number is 866,649:.\n\n- 8 (hundred thousands).\n- 6 (ten thousands).\n- 6 (thousands).\n- 6 (hundreds).\n- 4 (tens).\n- 9 (ones).\n\nWait, so the thousands place for the first number is the third digit from the left? Let me make sure:.\n\nBreaking down 921,126:.\n\nDigits: 9 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 6 (ones).\n\nSimilarly, 866,649:.\n\n8 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 6 (thousands), 6 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nSo when adding the thousands place: first number has 1, second has 6 → 1 +6 =7. Correct.\n\nThen ten thousands place: 2 +6=8. Correct.\n\nHundred thousands place: 9 +8=17 → write 7, carryover 1. So the million's place is 1.\n\nPutting all digits together: 1 (millions), 7 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 7 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 5 (ones). Wait, but when I write that out, it's 1,777,775? Wait, let me check the hundreds place again.\n\nWait, the hundreds place: first number's hundreds digit is 1, second is 6 → 1+6=7. Correct. Tens place was 2+4+1=7. Ones 15</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
