ting it all together, starting from the right: 6 (ones), 1 (tens), 0 (hundreds), 5 (thousands), 5 (ten-thousands), then 1 (hundred-thousands) plus the carried over 1 makes 11? Wait, maybe I messed up the hundred-thousands place. Let me check again.\n\nWait, let me retrace. The hundred-thousands digits are 6 and 5. 6+5 = 11, so we write down 1 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. Since there are no millions digits in the original numbers, the carried over 1 becomes the millions digit</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
