ber) + 1 (from second number) plus the carried over 1. That's 6 +1 +1 = 8. So 8 in the tens place, no carryover.\n\nHundreds place: 6 (first number) +5 (second number) = 11. So write down 1 and carry over 1.\n\nThousands place: 3 (first number) +4 (second number) + 1 (carryover) = 8. So 8, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands place: 6 (first number) +6 (second number) = 12. Write down 2, carry over 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 2 (first number) +7 (second number) +1 (carryover) = 10. So write down 0 and carry over 1. But since there are no more digits, that 1 is added as a new digit to the left.\n\nWait, let me go through each step carefully to avoid mistakes.\n\nStarting over step by step:.\n\nOnes place:.\n6 + 6 = 12. So record 2, carryover 1.\n\nTens place:.\n6 (from 263666) +1 (from 764516) = 7, plus the carryover 1 makes 8. So 8, no carryover.\n\nHundreds place:.\n6 +5 =11. So write 1, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place:.\n3 +4 =7, plus 1 carried over = 8. So 8, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands place:.\n6 +6 =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place:.\n2 +7 =9, plus the carried over 1 makes 10. Wait, wait, 2 +7 is 9, plus 1 is 10. So here, we write 0 and carry over 1. But since there are no more digits, that 1 becomes a new digit at the million's place</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
