messages:  [["Please act as a math teacher and solve the addition problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of <<number1>> and <<number2>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.\n1. The ones place: <<digit1>>\n2. The tens place: <<digit2>>\n<<other digits>>\nAnswer:\nTherefore, the final computed sum is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 731130 and 407746?\n# Reasoning:", "<think>\nOkay, so I need to add 731,130 and 407,746. Let me think about how to do this step by step. Hmm, maybe I should start by writing them down one on top of the other, aligning the digits properly. Let's see:.\n\nFirst, I'll line up the numbers by their place values. The rightmost digits are the ones place. .\n\nStarting with the ones place: 0 + 6. That should be 6. .\n\nNext, the tens place: 3 + 4. That's 7. .\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 1 + 7. Wait, 1 (from 731,130) and 7 (from 407,746). That adds up to 8. .\n\nNow the thousands place: 3 (from the first number) and 0 (from the second number). 3 + 0 is 3. .\n\nThen the ten-thousands place: 7 (from 731,130) and 4 (from 407,746). Hmm, 7 + 4 equals 11. Oh, that's more than 10, so I need to carry over. Let me write down 1 and carry over 1 to the next column. .\n\nWait, but actually, let me check the place values again. Maybe I messed up the positions. Let me write both numbers out with their place values to be sure.\n\nBreaking down each number:.\n\n731,130 is:.\n- 7 (hundred thousands).\n- 3 (ten thousands).\n- 1 (thousands).\n- 1 (hundreds).\n- 3 (tens).\n- 0 (ones).\n\n407,746 is:.\n- 4 (hundred thousands).\n- 0 (ten thousands).\n- 7 (thousands).\n- 7 (hundreds).\n- 4 (tens).\n- 6 (ones).\n\nWait, maybe I should stack them properly:.\n\n      731130.\n    + 407746.\n    -----------.\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 0 + 6 = 6.\n\nTens: 3 + 4 = 7.\n\nHundreds: 1 + 7 = 8.\n\nThousands: 1 (from first number's thousands place) + 7 (from second number's thousands place) = 8? Wait, no. Wait, let me check again.\n\nHold on, the first number is 731,130. Breaking it down:.\n\nDigits from right to left (starting at ones):.\n\n0 (ones).\n3 (tens).\n1 (hundreds).\n1 (thousands).\n3 (ten thousands).\n7 (hundred thousands).\n\nSecond number 407,746:.\n\n6 (ones).\n4 (tens).\n7 (hundreds).\n7 (thousands).\n0 (ten thousands).\n4 (hundred thousands).\n\nWait, that might be where I messed up earlier. Let me write them aligned properly:.\n\n          7 3 1 1 3 0.\n        + 4 0 7 7 4 6.\n        ---------------.\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n\nOnes: 0 + 6 = 6.\n\nTens: 3 + 4 = 7.\n\nHundreds: 1 (from first number) + 7 (from second) = 8.\n\nThousands place: 1 (first number's thousands digit) + 7 (second's thousands digit) = 8. .\n\nWait, the thousands digit of the first number is 1 (since 731,130 is 7 (hundred thousand), 3 (ten thousand), 1 (thousand), etc.), so yes. .\n\nTen thousands place: 3 (from first number) + 0 (second number's ten thousands place) = 3.\n\nHundred thousands place: 7 + 4 = 11. .\n\nWait, so putting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes:6.\n\nTens:7.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nHundred thousands:11. .\n\nWait, but 11 in the hundred thousands place? That would be 1,100,000. So actually, we need to carry over here. .\n\nWait, because 7 (hundred thousands from first number) + 4 (hundred thousands from second) = 11. So that's 11 hundred thousands, which is 1,100,000. So we can't have two digits there. So that 11 would mean we write 1 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. But since there's no millions in the original numbers, the carryover becomes a new digit. .\n\nTherefore, the total sum would be 1 (millions) 1 (hundred thousands) plus the rest. Let me recast all the digits with the carryover.\n\nLet me start over step by step with proper alignment and carryover:.\n\n          7 3 1 1 3 0.\n        + 4 0 7 7 4 6.\n        ---------------.\nStarting from the rightmost digit:.\n\nOnes place:.\n0 + 6 = 6. Write down 6.\n\nTens place:.\n3 + 4 = 7. Write down 7.\n\nHundreds place:.\n1 + 7 = 8. Write down 8.\n\nThousands place:.\n1 (from first number) +7 (from second) = 8. Write down 8.\n\nTen thousands place:.\n3 (from first) + 0 (from second) = 3. Write down 3.\n\nHundred thousands place:.\n7 (from first) +4 (from second) = 11. Here, we can't have 11 in a single digit, so we write down 1 and carry over 1 to the millions place. .\n\nSince there's no millions digits originally, the carried over 1 becomes the millions digit. .\n\nPutting it all together from right to left:.\n\nDigits:.\n\nOnes:6.\n\nTens:7.\n\nHundreds:8.\n\nThousands:8.\n\nTen thousands:3.\n\nHundred thousands:1 (with a carryover of 1 to millions).\n\nMillions:1.\n\nSo the total is 1 (million), 1 (hundred thousand), 3 (ten thousand), 8 (thousand), 8 (hundred), 7 (ten), 6 (one)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 14%|#4        | 72/500 [36:23<3:34:28, 30.07s/it]
