s place and carry over 1 to the ten thousands place.\n\nWait, hold on, maybe I messed up the places. Let me list the digits properly:.\n\nLet me write both numbers with commas for clarity:.\n\nFirst number: 881,994 → digits from right: 4 (ones), 9 (tens), 9 (hundreds), 1 (thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 8 (hundred thousands).\n\nSecond number: 359,660 → digits: 0 (ones), 6 (tens), 6 (hundreds), 9 (thousands), 5 (ten thousands), 3 (hundred thousands).\n\nSo aligning them:.\n\n       8 8 1 , 9 9 4.\n     + 3 5 9 , 6 6 0.\n     -----------------.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 4 + 0 = 4. Correct.\n\nTens place: 9 + 6 = 15. Write 5, carryover 1. Correct.\n\nHundreds place: 9 + 6 = 15, plus 1 is 16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 1 (from first number) + 9 (from second) = 10, plus 1 carried over is 11. So here, the thousands digit becomes 1, and carryover 1 to the next (ten thousands place).\n\nTen thousands place: 8 (from first) + 5 (from second) = 13, plus the carried over 1 makes 14. So write 4 in the ten thousands place and carryover 1 to the hundred thousands place.\n\nHundred thousands place: 8 + 3 = 11, plus the carried over 1 makes 12. So that's 2 in the hundred thousands and carryover 1 to the millions place. Since there's no higher digits, that 1 becomes the millions digit.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\nStarting from the right, the digits are:.\n\nOnes:4, Tens:5, Hundreds:6, Thousands:1, Ten thousands:4, Hundred thousands:2, and the carried over 1 in the millions place</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
