mber) plus the carried-over 1. So 7 + 6 is 13, plus 1 is 14. So we write 4 and carry over 1 again.\n\nTen-thousands place: 0 (from first number) + 8 (from second number) plus the carried-over 1. That's 0 +8 +1 =9. So 9, no carryover here.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 1 (from first number) + 2 (from second number) = 3. .\n\nPutting all the digits together: 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 4 (ones). Wait, let me check each step again to make sure I didn't mix up the places.\n\nWait, perhaps I should write this out step by step with each digit's place:.\n\nLet me list each digit position:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\n1. Ones place:.\nFirst number: 0.\nSecond number: 4.\nSum: 0 + 4 = 4. Write down 4.\n\n2. Tens place:.\nFirst number: 4.\nSecond number: 0.\nSum: 4 + 0 = 4. Write down 4.\n\n3. Hundreds place:.\nFirst number: 8.\nSecond number: 2.\nSum: 8 + 2 = 10. Write down 0, carry over 1.\n\n4. Thousands place:.\nFirst number:7, second number:6, plus carryover 1.\n7+6 =13, plus 1 =14. Write down 4, carry over 1.\n\n5. Ten-thousands place:.\nFirst number:0, second number:8, plus carryover 1.\n0+8=8 +1 =9. Write down 9, no carryover.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands place:.\nFirst number:1, second number:2. Sum 1+2=3. Write down 3.\n\nSo combining all the digits from right to left (since we started at ones):.\n\nThe digits are 3 (hundred thousands), 9 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 4 (tens), 4 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
