bers again.\n\nWait, maybe I misread the numbers. Let me confirm:.\n\nFirst number: 666,345. Breaking down the digits:.\n\n- Hundred thousands: 6.\n\n- Ten thousands: 6.\n\n- Thousands: 6.\n\n- Hundreds: 3.\n\n- Tens: 4.\n\n- Ones:5.\n\nSecond number: 753,776:.\n\n- Hundred thousands:7.\n\n- Ten thousands:5.\n\n- Thousands:3.\n\n- Hundreds:7.\n\n- Tens:7.\n\n- Ones:6.\n\nWait, so when adding the hundreds place: the first number's hundreds digit is 3, and the second's is 7. So 3 +7 =10, plus any carryover?.\n\nWait in the hundreds place, the previous step (tens place) had a carryover of 1, so let me retrace:.\n\nWait let's go step by step again:.\n\nOnes place: 5 +6 =11. So 1 written, carryover 1.\n\nTens place: 4 +7 =11, plus carryover 1 is 12. So 2 written, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 3 (from first number) +7 (from second) =10, plus carryover 1 is 11. So here, write 1 and carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 6 (from first number's thousands place) + 3 (from second's thousands place) =9, plus carryover 1 is 10. So write 0 and carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place: 6 (first's ten thousands) +5 (second's ten thousands) =11, plus carryover 1 is 12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands place: 6 +7 =13, plus carryover 1 is 14. So write 4 and carryover 1.\n\nNow, since there are no more digits, that final carryover 1 is added as a new digit at the front.\n\nPutting all the digits together from right to left:.\n\nOnes:1, Tens:2, Hundreds:1, Thousands:0, Ten thousands:2, Hundred thousands:4, plus the final 1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
