rom first number's thousands digit) plus 5 (second number's thousands digit) plus carryover 1 (from hundreds place) equals 11. So the thousands digit is 1 and carryover 1 to the ten-thousands. Then the ten-thousands digits are 8 +5 +1 (carryover) =14, so 4 there. So the digits from right to left are 0 (ones),0 (tens),3 (hundreds),1 (thousands),4 (ten thousands),9 (hundred thousands). Wait, no, the thousands place is 1, but the ten-thousands is 4. So putting it all together: 9 (hundred thousands), 4 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 0 (tens),0 (ones). So 941,300. Yes, that's correct. Okay, I think I got it right now. So the answer should be 941,300.\n\nBut let me check once more with another method. Let's add 485,989 +455,311.\n\nBreaking them into parts:\n\n485,989 + 455,311 = (400,000 + 80,000 +5,000 + 900 +80 +9) + (400,000 +50,000 +5,000 +300 +10 +1)\n\nAdding each part:\n\n400,000 +400,000 =800,000\n\n80,000 +50,000 =130,000 → total so far 930,000\n\n5,000 +5,000 =10,000 → total 940,000\n\n900 +300 =1,200 → 941,200\n\n80 +10 =90 → 941,290\n\n9 +1 =10 → 941,300.\n\nAh! There we go. So the total is indeed 941,300. Okay, so my initial confusion was because of miscalculating the steps, but after verifying with different methods, I'm confident the answer is 941,300. I need to make sure the reasoning in the template correctly reflects each digit's addition, including the carries over properly.\n</think>", '']
