 those gives 8 + 5 = 13. Since there's no higher place, we just write that as 13. Wait, but in terms of place value, that's 13 in the hundred-thousands place? Wait, maybe I need to check the positions again because sometimes I might have miscounted the places.\n\nWait, hold on, let me recount the digits to make sure I didn't misalign anything. Let's see:.\n\nThe first number is 816,730. Breaking it down:.\n- 8 (hundred thousands).\n- 1 (ten thousands).\n- 6 (thousands).\n- 7 (hundreds).\n- 3 (tens).\n- 0 (ones).\n\nThe second number is 568,080:.\n- 5 (hundred thousands).\n- 6 (ten thousands).\n- 8 (thousands).\n- 0 (hundreds).\n- 8 (tens).\n- 0 (ones).\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\nOnes: 0+0=0.\nTens: 3+8=11 (carry 1).\nHundreds:7+0+1=8.\nThousands:6+8=14 (carry 1).\nTen-thousands:1+6+1=8.\nHundred-thousands:8+5=13.\n\nWait, so the hundred-thousands place adds to 13, so that would be 3 in the hundred-thousands and carry over 1 to the millions place? Oh right! I almost forgot. Since 8 (hundred thousands) +5 (hundred thousands) is 13 hundred thousands, which is 1,300,000. So that's actually a 1 in the millions place and 3 in the hundred thousands</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
