r 1 from the ten-thousands place. So 12 + 1 = 13. Therefore, the hundred-thousands place will be 3, and we need to carry over 1 to the millions place. Wait, but the original numbers don't have a digit in the millions place. So that carried-over 1 becomes the new digit in the millions place.\n\nPutting it all together, starting from the right: the digits we've calculated are 9 (ones), 6 (tens), 8 (hundreds), 3 (thousands), 0 (ten-thousands), 3 (hundred-thousands), and then the carried-over 1 in the millions place</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
