million place. Oh right, so the total becomes 1,085,107?.\n\nWait a second, let me go through each step again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nNumbers aligned:.\n\n      4 1 9 6 0 5.\n    + 6 6 5 4 9 2.\n    ---------------.\nStarting from the right (ones place):.\n5 + 2 = 7. Good.\n\nTens place: 0 + 9 = 9. That's straightforward.\n\nHundreds place: 6 + 4 = 10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\nThousands place: 9 + 5 = 14, plus the 1 is 15. So 5 here, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 1 (from first number) + 6 (from second number) = 7, plus 1 carried over is 8. Correct.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 4 + 6 = 10. So here, 10. But since there's no higher place, we have to add that as a new digit. So the total would be 1 (million) 0 (hundred-thousands) followed by the rest?.\n\nWait, the digits would be:.\n\nThe carried over 1 from the hundred-thousands addition (10) becomes the millions place. So the digits are:.\n\nMillions place: 1.\n\nHundred-thousands: 0.\n\nTen-thousands: 8.\n\nThousands:5.\n\nHundreds:0.\n\nTens:9.\n\nOnes:7</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
