d thousands. So that's equivalent to 1 million and 1 hundred thousand. So you would write down 1 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. But since there's no millions place in the original numbers, the carryover becomes a new digit at the beginning.\n\nSo the total would be 1 (million) plus 1 (hundred thousand) plus the rest. So the digits would be:\n\nMillions place: 1,\n\nHundred thousands: 1,\n\nTen thousands:6,\n\nThousands:3,\n\nHundreds:3,\n\nTens:2,\n\nOnes:7.\n\nSo putting that together: 1,163,327.\n\nWait, let me confirm with another method. Let's add the numbers step by step.\n\n949,935 + 213,392.\n\nBreak it down:\n\nFirst, add 949,935 + 200,000 = 1,149,935.\n\nThen add 13,392: 1,149,935 + 13,392.\n\n1,149,935 + 10,000 = 1,159,935.\n\nThen +3,392: 1,159,935 + 3,392 = 1,163,327.\n\nYes, that matches. So the final answer is 1,163,327.\n\nTherefore, the reasoning steps should be:\n\nOnes:5+2=7.\n\nTens:3+9=12 (carry 1).\n\nHundreds:9+3+1=13 (carry 1).\n\nThousands:9+3+1=13 (carry 1).\n\nTen thousands:4+1+1=6.\n\nHundred thousands:9+2=11 (carryover 1 to millions place).\n\nSo the digits would be 1 (millions), 1 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 7 (ones).\n\nHence, 1,163,327.\n\nI think that's correct. Let me check with calculator math.\n\n949,935 + 213,392.\n\n949,935 + 200,000 is 1,149,935.\n\nAdding 13,392: 1,149,935 + 13,392 = 1,163,327. Yep, that's correct.\n\nSo the answer is 1,163,327.\n</think>\n\n",
