 digit is 9, and hundreds is 7. So now, moving to tens place:\n\nTens digits:5 +3 =8. No carryover. So that's 8 tens.\n\nOnes place:7 +6=13. That's 3 ones and carryover 1 to tens. Wait, but we already did the tens place. So here's the issue again. If you go left to right, you have to adjust previous digits when a carryover occurs from the right. That complicates things, which is why right-to-left is standard.\n\nBut in my previous left-to-right breakdown, I treated each place independently, but actually, you can't do that. So the right-to-left method is more straightforward for handling carries.\n\nAnyway, both methods when done correctly give the same result, 499,793. Let me confirm with another approach, like using rounding.\n\n204,857 + 294,936.\n\nLet's round 204,857 to 205,000. That's an increase of 143.\n\nAnd 294,936 to 295,000, which is an increase of 64.\n\nAdding the rounded numbers: 205,000 +295,000 =500,000.\n\nNow subtract the total amount we added: 143 +64 =207.\n\nSo 500,000 -207 =499,793. Perfect, matches again. So that's a good check.\n\nTherefore, I'm confident the answer is 499,793.\n\nBut let me just write out the addition vertically again to ensure no digit was misplaced:\n\n\u2003204857\n\n+294936\n\n----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes:7+6=13 \u2192 3, carryover 1.\n\nTens:5+3=8 +1=9.\n\nHundreds:8+9=17 \u21927, carryover1.\n\nThousands:4+4=8 +1=9.\n\nTen-thousands:0+9=9.\n\nHundred-thousands:2+2=4.\n\nPutting all together: 4 9 9 7 9 3 \u2192 499,793. Yes, that's correct.\n\nI think that's thorough. The key steps were aligning the digits properly and carefully handling the carries, especially in the hundreds and thousands places. The cross-check with rounding helped confirm the result. So the final answer should be 499,793.\n\n</think>\n\n",
