, 7 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 0 (ones). Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo back to thousands place: first number is 8 (thousands place), second is 1 (thousands place). So 8 +1 =9, plus the carried over 1 makes 10. So we write 0 in the thousands place and carry over 1 again.\n\nNext is the ten thousands place. First number has 4, second has 7. 4 +7 =11, plus the carried 1 is 12. So write 2 in ten thousands place and carry over 1.\n\nFinally the hundred thousands place: first number has 2, second has 6. 2+6=8, plus the carried over 1 makes 9. So the hundred thousands place is 9.\n\nPutting it all together, the digits from right to left are:.\n\nOnes:2, tens:5, hundreds:5, thousands:0, ten thousands:2, hundred thousands:9. Wait, but wait, let me check again because when I added the thousands place, I had 8+1 +1 (carry) =10. So thousands digit is 0, carryover 1 to ten thousands. Then ten thousands place was 4+7=11 +1=12. So ten thousands is 2, carryover 1. Then hundred thousands 2+6=8 +1=9</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
