r 391,106:\n\n- 3: hundred thousands (100,000)\n\n- 9: ten thousands (10,000)\n\n- 1: thousands (1,000)\n\n- 1: hundreds (100)\n\n- 0: tens (10)\n\n- 6: ones (1)\n\nSimilarly for 766,404:\n\n-7: hundred thousands.\n\n-6: ten thousands.\n\n-6: thousands.\n\n-4: hundreds.\n\n-0: tens.\n\n-4: ones.\n\nSo when adding digit by digit from right (ones):\n\nOnes:6 +4 =10 \u21920, carryover 1.\n\nTens:0 +0 +1 =1 \u21921, no carryover.\n\nHundreds:1 (from first number's hundreds place) +4 (second's hundreds) =5 \u21925.\n\nThousands:1 (first's thousands) +6 (second's thousands) =7 \u21927.\n\nTen thousands:9 (first's ten thousands) +6 (second's ten thousands) =15 \u21925, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands:3 (first) +7 (second) +1 (carryover) =11 \u219211.\n\nSo the digits from right to left are:\n\nOnes:0\n\nTens:1\n\nHundreds:5\n\nThousands:7\n\nTen thousands:5\n\nHundred thousands:11.\n\nSo the total is 1,157,510. That seems correct.\n\nWait, but when writing the number, the hundred thousands place having 11 would actually be represented as 1 in the millions place and 1 in the hundred thousands. So actually, the carryover from the hundred thousands place would need to go to the millions place. Wait a second, I think I made an error here.\n\nAh! Here's a mistake I almost made. When adding the hundred thousands digits (3 +7) plus the carryover 1 from the ten thousands place, the sum is 11. Since the hundred thousands place is the sixth digit, getting 11 means we have to carry over to the millions place. So the hundred thousands place would be 1 (since 11 divided by 10 is 1 with a remainder of 1?), no, wait, actually:\n\nWait, the hundred thousands place is the sixth digit from the right. If the total there is 11, then 11 in the hundred thousands place would actually require a carryover to the millions place. Because 11 in that place would mean 11 * 100,000, which is 1,100,000. So the correct way is:\n\nWhen you have 3 (hundred thousands) +7 (hundred thousands) +1 (carryover) =11 hundred thousands. To represent this, you write down 1 in the hundred thousands place and carryover 1 to the millions place. Since there was no millions digit before, this becomes a new digit at the front.\n\nTherefore, the final digits would be:\n\nMillions place:1 (from the carryover).\n\nHundred thousands place:1 (since 11 -10 =1).\n\nThen the rest as before: ten thousands:5, thousands:7, hundreds:5, tens:1, ones:0.\n\nSo the number becomes 1,157,510. Which is the same as before, but the carryover into the millions place is important. So the final answer is indeed 1,157,510.\n\nOkay, so I think that's correct. Let me verify with another method: using expanded form.\n\n391,106 = 300,000 + 90,000 + 1,000 + 100 + 0 +6.\n\n766,404 = 700,000 + 60,000 + 6,000 + 400 + 0 +4.\n\nAdding each part:\n\n300,000 +700,000 =1,000,000.\n\n90,000 +60,000=150,000.\n\n1,000 +6,000=7,000.\n\n100 +400=500.\n\n0+0=0.\n\n6+4=10.\n\nNow sum all these:\n\n1,000,000 +150,000 =1,150,000.\n\n+7,000 \u21921,157,000.\n\n+500 \u21921,157,500.\n\n+0 \u2192 same.\n\n+10 \u21921,157,510.\n\nYes, that's the same result. So the answer is 1,157,510.\n\nI think that's solid. Let me just do a quick calculator check in my mind: 391k + 766k is roughly 1,157,000, and then the remaining 106 +404 is 510, so total is 1,157,510. Perfect, that matches. So I'm confident the answer is correct.\n\n</think>\n\n",
