s), 2 (tens), 0 (ones). Wait, so in the ten-thousands place, both numbers have 9 and 9. So adding those: 9 + 9 = 18, plus the carryover 1? Wait, no, wait. Wait, the thousands place addition gave us a carryover of 1 to the ten-thousands place. Wait, let me retrace.\n\nWait, let's go step by step again to make sure I didn't mix up the places.\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me break down each number digit by digit:.\n\nFirst number: 698,980.\n\nBreaking down each digit from right to left (starting at ones):.\n\n- Ones: 0.\n- Tens: 8.\n- Hundreds: 9.\n- Thousands: 8.\n- Ten-thousands: 9.\n- Hundred-thousands: 6.\n\nSecond number: 495,520.\n\nBreaking down similarly:.\n\n- Ones: 0.\n- Tens: 2.\n- Hundreds: 5.\n- Thousands: 5.\n- Ten-thousands: 9.\n- Hundred-thousands: 4.\n\nSo, when adding, let's align each place correctly:.\n\nStarting with Ones:.\n\n0 + 0 = 0. Correct.\n\nTens: 8 + 2 = 10. So 0 with carryover 1. That's right.\n\nHundreds: 9 (from first number) + 5 (from second) + 1 (carryover) = 15. So 5 and carryover 1. Correct.\n\nThousands place: 8 (first number) +5 (second number) +1 (carryover) = 14. So 4 and carryover 1. Wait, but the thousands digits are 8 and 5? Wait, yes, first number has 8 in thousands place, second has 5. So 8+5=13, plus the carryover 1 makes 14? Wait, 8+5 is 13, plus 1 is 14. So yes, 4 and carryover 1. So that's correct.\n\nTen-thousands place: Now, ten-thousands digits are 9 (first number) and 9 (second number). Adding those: 9 +9 = 18, plus the carryover 1 (from thousands place) is 19. So that would be 9 in the ten-thousands place and carryover 1 to the hundred-thousands place.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 6 (first number) +4 (second number) +1 (carryover) = 11. So that's 1 and 1, meaning 11 in the hundred-thousands place? Wait, but how does that work?.\n\nWait, let me write all the digits step by step again:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 0 + 0 = 0.\n\nTens: 8 +2 =10 → 0, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:9 +5 +1 =15 → 5, carryover 1.\n\nThousands:8 +5 +1 =14 →4, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands:9 +9 +1 =19 →9, carryover 1.\n\nHundred-thousands:6 +4 +1 =11 →1 (hundred-thousands place) and carryover 1 to the millions place.\n\nWait, so the total sum would have a millions digit of 1, then hundred-thousands digit 1, ten-thousands 9, thousands 4, hundreds 5, tens 0, ones 0.\n\nPutting it all together:.\n\n1 (millions), 1 (hundred-thousands), 9 (ten-thousands), 4 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 0 (tens), 0 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
