ce.\n\nFinally, the hundred thousands place: 7 and 6. 7 + 6 = 13. Wait, so that's 13. Hmm, so we need to write down 3 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. Since there's no higher digits, that carried over 1 becomes a 1 in the millions place.\n\nPutting it all together: the millions place is 1, then 3 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 4 (tens), and 1 (ones). Wait, let me check each step again to be sure.\n\nWait a second, let me go through each digit again step by step to confirm.\n\nStarting over:.\n\nOnes place:.\n2 (from 716,122) + 9 (from 623,619) = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTens place:.\n2 (from first number's tens) + 1 (second number's tens) = 3, plus the carryover 1 is 4. Correct, so 4 in tens.\n\nHundreds place:.\n1 (first number's hundreds) + 6 (second's hundreds) = 7. So 7 there.\n\nThousands place:.\n6 (first's thousands) + 3 (second's thousands) = 9. That's right.\n\nTen thousands place:.\n1 (first's ten thousands) + 2 (second's ten thousands) = 3. Okay.\n\nHundred thousands place:.\n7 (first's hundred thousands) + 6 (second's hundred thousands) = 13. So write 3 and carryover 1</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
