ng it all together: 1 (million), 2 (hundred thousand), 3 (ten thousand), 2 (thousand), 7 (hundred), 9 (ten), 9 (one). So that's 1,232,799? Wait, wait no. Wait, the thousands place was 2, so the digits from left to right would be 1 (million), 2 (hundred thousand), 3 (ten thousand), 2 (thousand), 7 (hundred), 9 (ten), 9 (one). That is 1,232,799. Wait, but earlier I thought I had 1,232,809. Wait, so where did I go wrong?\n\nWait in the first calculation I thought the ten thousands place was 3 and the hundred thousands was 2 with a carryover 1. So the digits are:\n\nMillions place:1\n\nHundred thousands:2\n\nTen thousands:3\n\nThousands:2\n\nHundreds:7\n\nTens:9\n\nOnes:9\n\nSo that's 1,232,799. But in my first reasoning I had written 1,232,809. So that was a mistake in the initial thought process. The correct total should be 1,232,799.\n\nWait, but then in my first method I must have miscalculated the thousands place. Let me see again:\n\nThousands place in first number is 7 (since the number is 767,914: 7 (hundred thousand), 6 (ten thousand), 7 (thousand), 9 (hundred), etc.), and in the second number it's 4 (from 464,885: 4 (hundred thousand),6 (ten thousand),4 (thousand)...). So 7 +4 +1 (carry from hundreds) is 12. So that's 2 in thousands place, carryover 1. Then ten thousands: 6+6+1=13. So 3 there, carryover 1. Then hundred thousands:7+4+1=12. So 2 there, carryover 1. So total is 1,232,799. So my initial reasoning had an error when I first thought 1,232,809. Probably a typo when I was writing the first answer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be 1,232,799. Let me verify with another method: using calculator-style addition.\n\nLet me add 767,914 +464,885:\n\n767,914\n\n+464,885\n\nStart adding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4+5=9\n\n1+8=9\n\n9+8=17 (7, carryover1)\n\n7 (thousands place first number) +4 (thousands place second) +1=12 (2, carryover1)\n\n6 (ten thousands) +6 (ten thousands) +1=13 (3, carryover1)\n\n7 (hundred thousand) +4 (hundred thousand) +1=