nHundreds: 9 + 0 =9 +1 =10. So 0, carryover 1.\n\nThousands: 3 (from first number's thousands place?) Wait, hold on. Wait, let me check the digits again. Wait, maybe I misaligned the numbers. Let me write each digit properly.\n\nWait, 683,939 is 6 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 9 (ones).\n\nThe second number is 104,063: 1 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 6 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nSo when adding:.\n\nHundred thousands: 6 + 1 =7.\n\nTen thousands: 8 +0 =8.\n\nThousands:3 +4=7.\n\nHundreds:9 +0=9.\n\nTens:3 +6=9.\n\nOnes:9 +3=12.\n\nWait, but that can't be right because when I added earlier, there were carryovers. Wait, maybe I need to realign. Wait, perhaps I made an error in the positions. Let me write them properly aligned:.\n\n       6 8 3 9 3 9.\n     + 1 0 4 0 6 3.\n     ----------------.\nAdding each column from right to left:.\n\nOnes: 9 +3 =12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nTens: 3 +6 =9 plus 1 carried is 10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nHundreds:9 +0 =9 plus 1=10. Write 0, carryover1.\n\nThousands:3 (from first number) +4 (second)=7 plus 1 carried =8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands:8 +0 =8.\n\nHundred thousands:6 +1=7.\n\nWait, so putting it all together from right to left:.\n\nThe digits would be 2 (ones), 0 (tens), 0 (hundreds), 8 (thousands),8 (ten thousands),7 (hundred thousands)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
