ait, maybe I messed up the places. Let me check again. Let me write the numbers with their place values:.\n\n533,481 is 5 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\n608,480 is 6 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nAdding them:.\n\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes: 1 + 0 =1.\n\nTens: 8 +8 =16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:4 +4 +1=9.\n\nThousands:3 (from first number) +8 (second number) = 11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nWait, the thousands place in the first number is 3 (since it's 533,481: the thousands digit is the fourth from the right, which is 3). The second number's thousands digit is 8 (from 608,480: the fourth digit from the right is 8). So 3 +8 =11. So yes, that gives 1 in thousands place and carryover 1.\n\nThen ten-thousands place: 3 (from first number, which is the second digit from the left, so ten thousands place) plus 0 (from second number's ten thousands place) plus the carried over 1. So 3+0+1=4. Wait, wait a second, maybe I messed up the place positions earlier.\n\nHold on, let me re-express the numbers with each digit's place:.\n\nFirst number: 5 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 1 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 6 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 8 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 0 (ones).\n\nAdding:.\n\nHundred thousands: 5 +6 =11.\n\nTen thousands:3 +0 =3.\n\nThousands:3 +8 =11.\n\nHundreds:4 +4 =8.\n\nTens:8 +8 =16.\n\nOnes:1 +0 =1.\n\nWait, so perhaps I need to structure it properly. Let me try adding again step by step, maybe I messed up the order earlier.\n\nLet me write them vertically:.\n\n      5 3 3 4 8 1.\n    + 6 0 8 4 8 0.\n    ----------------.\nStarting from the right (ones):.\n\nOnes:1 +0 =1. Write 1.\n\nTens:8 +8 =16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:4 +4 =8 plus the carried 1 =9. Write 9.\n\nThousands:3 (from first) +8 (second) =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen-thousands:3 (first) +0 (second) =3 plus carried 1 =4. Write 4.\n\nHundred-thousands:5 +6 =11. Write 11.\n\nSo putting it all together, the sum is 11 (hundred thousands) 4 (ten thousands) 1 (thousands) 9 (hundreds) 6 (tens) 1 (ones)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]] 75%|#######4  | 373/500 [3:28:52<56:08, 26.52s/it]
