n3,000 (thousands place is 3, so 3,000)\n\n600 (hundreds)\n\n90 (tens)\n\n0 (ones).\n\nSecond number breakdown:\n\n800,000 (hundred thousands)\n\n10,000 (ten thousands)\n\n1,000 (thousands)\n\n500 (hundreds)\n\n0 (tens)\n\n9 (ones).\n\nNow adding each place:\n\nHundred thousands: 100,000 +800,000 =900,000\n\nTen thousands: 0 +10,000 =10,000\n\nThousands: 3,000 +1,000 =4,000\n\nHundreds:600 +500=1,100\n\nTens:90 +0=90\n\nOnes:0 +9=9\n\nNow add all these together:\n\n900,000 +10,000 =910,000\n\n910,000 +4,000 =914,000\n\n914,000 +1,100 =915,100\n\n915,100 +90 =915,190\n\n915,190 +9 =915,199. Yep, that's correct.\n\nSo the final answer is 915,199. I must have miscalculated my first time when I thought 915,200. Probably added the hundreds place as 6+5=11, then carried over but maybe miscounted the other digits. So the correct answer is definitely 915,199.\n\nWait but in the reasoning steps above, when I listed the digits:\n\nAfter hundreds place: 1 (with carryover). Then thousands place 3+1+1=5, which is correct. Then ten thousands 0+1=1. Hundred thousands 1+8=9. So putting all digits: 9 (hundred thousands), 1 (ten thousands), 5 (thousands), 1 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 9 (ones). So 915,199. Yes, that's right.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 915,199.\n\n</think>\n\n",
