ding them gives 11. So I write down 1 in the thousands place and carry over 1 to the ten thousands place.\n\nTen thousands place: the digits are 0 (from 807,249) and 7 (from 874,075). Adding those gives 7, plus the carried over 1 makes 8. So that's 8 in the ten thousands place. No carryover here.\n\nNow the hundred thousands place: 8 and 8. Adding those gives 16. So we write down 6 in the hundred thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions place. Wait, but since both numbers only have up to hundred thousands, this carryover becomes a new digit in the millions place. So that's 1 in the millions place.\n\nPutting all the digits together: the millions place is 1, then 6 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 1 (thousands), 3 (hundreds), 2 (tens), and 4 (ones). Wait a second, let me verify each step again because sometimes when adding large numbers, it's easy to miscount the places.\n\nWait, let me write this out step by step systematically:.\n\nLet me align them properly:.\n\n        807249.\n      +874075.\n      ---------.\nStarting from the right:.\n\nOnes place: 9 + 5 = 14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\nTens place: 4 +7 =11 +1=12. Write 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds place: 2 +0 =2 +1=3. Write 3, no carryover.\n\nThousands place:7 +4=11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\nTen thousands place:0 +7=7 +1=8. Write 8.\n\nHundred thousands place:8 +8=16. Write 6, carryover 1.\n\nMillions place: The carryover 1. So we have 1 in the millions place</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
